<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamakhya Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kamakhya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kamakhya Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to the mother goddess Kamakhya on Nilachal Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. One of the most important centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shakta</a> and Tantric worship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, it was patronised by the kings of Kamarupa. After its destruction in the early 16th century it was rebuilt in 1565 by the Koch king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a>, and it draws huge crowds to the yearly Ambubachi festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple.jpg" alt="Kamakhya Temple" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Devkmaravi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamakhya_Temple_-_DEV_8829.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
